/ref 17592186129563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129083 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129083 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129083 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129083 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129083 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129083 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129083 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129083 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129083 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129083 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129083 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129363 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129363 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129363 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129363 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129363 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129363 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129363 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129363 #edb/ref 1045 "Extending the Pythagorean Identity to All Quadrants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129363 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186129916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129363 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129363 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186129363 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8bd2ec07-dfc3-530f-b400-3bce1febe87e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129821 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129821 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129821 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/39/39f62ad81b496c06c2e89ee01110ca2ad1873512056f50daf13348d3906a05c9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\cos θ = \\frac{1}{2}$, what is the value of $\\sin θ$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129821 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e01c4cb-dc31-5c31-9aea-ee2290d20500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129894 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129894 #edb/ref 1061 11659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129894 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e644585d-876a-5a47-879b-800ee7c06ead" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129894 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129895 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129895 #edb/ref 1061 11661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129895 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "198ba7a9-15e3-54ae-9cf7-a657501742b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129895 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129896 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129896 #edb/ref 1061 11664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129896 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40fae29f-2e89-52fe-9afd-d197bd48df89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129896 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129897 #edb/ref 1061 11666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129897 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c5d06736-c0a2-5014-b8ec-523edb383b95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129897 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129916 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129916 #edb/ref 1061 13319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129916 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba029153-c2b2-5d85-9274-f3a6cf3e00a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129916 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129917 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129917 #edb/ref 1061 13322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129917 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "169bc7d9-7551-55a5-9cfd-107f346c1bed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129917 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130332 #edb/ref 1064 "For any point on the unit circle, we can draw a right triangle whose hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point.\n\n![](images/b1/b1ec08957fd0d09179853d6d56c20b7058c4531bdd7e766c5b3462ba246fce2b.png)\n\nEach hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle and consequently has a length equal to $1.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of *any* point on the unit circle must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this fact to find one coordinate of a point on the unit circle given the other.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$ in the fourth quadrant on the unit circle is $\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{3}{7}$ into the above and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t          \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty&= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}}\\[5pt]\n\t        y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n\n}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the *fourth* quadrant, $y$ must be *negative*. So we select the negative answer. Therefore, $y=-\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130332 #edb/ref 1065 2720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130332 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "123bbfdb-b303-5c97-b319-aea0e9be6205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130332 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130333 #edb/ref 1064 "By the Pythagorean theorem, we know that the equation \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = 1\n$$\n\nholds for any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle. \n\nNow, we also know that for any point on the unit circle, the coordinates $(x,y)$ are related to the central angle $\\theta$ via the formulas\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Pythagorean Identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equation is called the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity**, and it holds for *every* angle $\\theta.$ This includes negative angles and angles that are larger than one full rotation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130333 #edb/ref 1065 2721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130333 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2adb34b1-9f8d-5f02-bafd-029ac9251084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130333 #edb/ref 1067 "The Pythagorean Identity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256879 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{10\\sqrt{19}}{19}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256879 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256879 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a7d951e-522f-5c0c-b140-5420f9cea5fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257548 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =10\\sqrt{14}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257548 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257548 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "585745f7-471d-5057-b7de-1f59ab9b9e00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257587 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257587 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257587 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5cb55744-884c-5771-87b6-d73c7c9ed2ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257838 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257838 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257838 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "773ad8ec-a27e-55f5-aa77-2b5b2d320281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258143 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{10\\sqrt{19}}{19}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258143 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258143 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9af4b374-5811-59d7-b0e2-35660f0ef4ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259327 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186256879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259327 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259327 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259327 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259327 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259327 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259327 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259327 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186258143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259327 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a6a5f3ef-0107-5f95-a46a-ebfa1923c402" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259764 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259764 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259764 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259764 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{9}{10}$ and ${90}^{∘}<\\theta <{180}^{∘}$ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259764 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "792a4ca6-6d8c-5305-a32b-2dade065ed1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259764 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/82/82ad5587912af9d07bf447281bba3eb714bfdfdf38fef529529956912b0a77ec.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{9}{10}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{9}{10}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{9}{10})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{81}{100}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{19}{100}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{10}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{10})}=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{19}}=\\frac{10\\sqrt{19}}{19}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326990 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326990 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326990 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0697fb32-13a8-5152-929c-1cbc92697a41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327292 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{25}{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327292 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327292 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "29cbd9c5-1f6a-5f93-bfe0-6e1833448eef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327771 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327771 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327771 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f59d2b0-87ec-535c-98db-46f721956829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328098 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{25}{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328098 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328098 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "875b0e21-3bdb-537e-83a7-6070520501cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328139 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328139 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328139 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8c78c851-07e7-51bc-a294-83ea44fc4afa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186326990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329354 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329354 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329354 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329354 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186327292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329354 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6067e08a-a253-5424-8dcb-5049856ea945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329770 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329770 #edb/ref 1069 "q-45875" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329770 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329770 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329770 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c5295f76-3e41-52a4-88d5-2769f2995c21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329770 #edb/ref 1074 "As $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nLet's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/aa/aaba5af0542d29ef91d4b0e17907a81e32bccc402c48b32c205e7298630b5bc0.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ we have:\n\n- $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{5})}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(-\\frac{4}{5})}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4}=-\\frac{25}{12}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351602 #edb/ref 1080 "csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{15}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351602 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351602 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03cea74b-49d9-5194-911a-8fb6c23a0296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351676 #edb/ref 1080 "csc⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{16}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351676 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351676 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d8ad358-acae-56e0-a279-d1a3dec16c18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351885 #edb/ref 1080 "csc⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{15}}{15}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351885 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351885 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "232c19ba-9a6c-5c2f-ab92-abe46b6581b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352499 #edb/ref 1080 "csc⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352499 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352499 #edb/r