2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is negative in the fourth quadrant. So $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncsc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4})}=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{15}}=-\\frac{4\\sqrt{15}}{15}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
